star trek picard meets deep space nine  in the first episode of season two and  that's not the only easter egg buried in  this reference heavy show  the premiere episode of picard season  two opens with starfleet officers  scurrying through the corridors in the  middle of what appears to be a battle  aboard a starship arriving on the bridge  things aren't looking good as an unseen  villain is tapping into the ship's  systems and appears to be gaining  control unable to overpower the enemy  picard commands the ship's computer to  activate the auto-destruct sequence we  later learn the enemy is the borg and  long time fans will note this isn't the  first time that picard has had to order  self-destruct due to a borg invasion it  is however a major change from his  previous behavior and shows he's learned  a lesson from a certain former klingon  security chief in the 1996 film star  trek first contact the borg had  infiltrated the enterprise and had  controlled more than half the ship  picard's trusted officer and friend  lieutenant commander wharf recommended  they self-destruct the ship in order to  prevent its total takeover picard  adamantly refused to destroy it and it  wasn't until he got a lecture from lily  that he relented and set the ship to  self-destruct this time it seems picard  is much quicker to respond having  learned that when the borg take over  sometimes it's best to activate the  ship's auto destruct system right away  as many eagle-dyed viewers noticed in  the first teaser for the new season a  major piece of deep space nine lore  appeared prominently on the table in  picard's library it appeared that the  former enterprise captain an avid  archaeologist was studying the reckoning  tablet which originally made its first  and only appearance in star trek deep  space nine  i see you recognize it in the episode  the reckoning the tablet was smashed to  pieces by captain cisco after he  received a vision in doing so he  released the villainous parades that  were locked in a centuries-long war with  the bajoran prophets many wondered if  the appearance of the tablet was an  easter egg just for trailers perhaps as  a simple reference to star trek deep  space nine but the tablet does indeed  appear in the opening episode of picard  season two as picard and laris speak in  his library it appears on the table  between them in the center of the screen  and it is still in pieces there's a  viewing glass above it which suggests  picard has indeed been studying it given  its prominence in the scene we wonder if  it's more than just an easter egg and  perhaps a hint of more to come  as the first episode transitions to  picard's speech at starfleet academy we  see picard in his new role as chancellor  of the academy standing before a group  of incoming cadets behind him is a  series of flags presumably a federation  member worlds that's notable because  there's a number of flags from worlds  who weren't members of the federation  during the next generation while we do  see flags for the tellerites and vulcans  who were founding members we see a  klingon flag as well long-time allies of  the federation since at least the days  of tng it seems they could now be full  members of the ufp we also see the  bajoran flag indicating that bajor may  have finally gained membership following  deep space nine but the most curious  flag is the ferengi flag though  ferenganar was on its way to change with  the appointment of the less greed  focused rom as their grand nagis at the  end of deep space nine it's curious that  they'd become federation members so  quickly a new foreigner needs a new kind  of [ __ ] kinder gentler [ __ ] and  that's you my boy if these aren't the  flags of federation members then it's  possible these are simply flags of  worlds from which starfleet academy has  found cadets meaning the ferengi flag is  a reference to the character nog the  young ferengi who attended the academy  and served aboard the defiant on deep  space nine  during his speech picard is proud to  introduce his friend elenor as the first  fully romulan entrant into starfleet  academy this indicates that at some  point prior to the events of the episode  the academy had admitted other cadets  with at least partial romulan ancestry  it's notable also because there has been  at least one starfleet officer that  we've seen whose bloodline could be  traced to romulans a crewman named simon  tarsus from the next generation episode  the drum head tarsus was investigated  for his possible role in sabotaging the  enterprise but his real crime was lying  about his parentage by claiming he was  part vulcan rather than romulan the  paternal grandfather of whom you speak  was not a vulcan but it was in fact a  romulan  that it is romulan blood you carry  ultimately tarsus did not even attend  the academy and chose to enlist instead  nevertheless it was heavily implied that  there was great controversy surrounding  officers who carried the blood of a  federation enemy it remains to be seen  if this will be an issue for elnor who  vocalizes his concerns about fitting in  at the academy due to his race though  picard's gift a book written by spock  should help  the picard season 2 premiere gives us a  surprising glimpse into the childhood of  john luke picard and hints that  something terrible may have happened to  his mother when he was young but it's  his mother's inspiring words to the  young picard as he looked up into the  night sky that are the source of  character drama in this episode in  flashbacks we see how yvette picard  nurtured her son's love of the stars  offering to build him an observatory of  sorts on the family estate we know from  the tng episode family that picard's  father and brother both decried his  choice to enter starfleet so it seems a  fitting revelation that it was his  mother who helped ignite his exploratory  spirit but in his speech to the academy  picard chooses to quote his mother with  a phrase that clearly stuck with him all  of his life let's see what's out there  it's an inspiring moment and one that  affected picard greatly and die hard  trekkies will remember this line for its  appearance in the tng premiere episode  encounter at farpoint let's see what's  out there  [Music]  engage  back at the academy cadets await their  first assignments with an officer  calling out names and ships for them to  report to one cadet is sent to the  hikaru sulu clearly named for the  helmsman of the original uss enterprise  originally played by george takei in  star trek generations we met sulu's  daughter who helmed the enterprise bee  and it's clear from this new starship  that her father's name has carried a  powerful legacy even into the 25th  century other ships include the  excelsior captained by rafi where  eleanor is assigned and the uss grissom  the original excelsior was captained by  sulu himself in star trek vi the  undiscovered country while it wouldn't  be unprecedented if eleanor's ship were  the same excelsior it's most likely a  newer ship with the same name the  grissom meanwhile though not seen was  the name of the science vessel that  carried lieutenant savic and david  marcus to the genesis planet in star  trek iii the search for spock admiral  picard also mentions to rafi his  intention to make some modifications to  the kobayashi meru test which is  designed to evaluate an officer's  decision-making skills in a no-win  situation this test has been seen or  referenced several times most famously  in star trek ii the wrath of khan but  again in jj abram's star trek reboot  when picard visits his old friend gainen  who is now attending a bar in los  angeles he goes to an address that will  make fans of the next generation smile  10 forward street fans will recall the  location of guidance bar on the  enterprise as 10 forward but that's  hardly the only easter egg behind the  bar we see bottles of cardassian canar  from deep space nine also on the wall is  designed for our conus lager first seen  in star trek iii the search for spock  when the two sit down for a drink she  offers up some options but selects a  bottle of soari and brandy a classic  trek ale seen many times throughout the  franchise here she refers to it as hooch  seeming to reveal the drink is something  less than a classy top shelf spirit  when they first meet picard notes that  guidance people the alloryans age slowly  and she tells him that aging is  something that happens only if we choose  to and that she has chosen to for the  benefit of humans who dislike being  reminded of their own mortality this  helps explain to audiences why the  centuries-old gainen played by whoopi  goldberg looks 20 years older than the  last time we saw her in star trek  nemesis she then makes a few clever nods  to classic tng episodes referencing the  times that they stood side by side to  face a new galactic threat like they did  with the borg in the episode cue who she  also references the events of  yesterday's enterprise when she recalls  the time that they jumped universes  together but picard hasn't come to  reminisce and is instead looking for  some advice regarding his love life  picard insists that he has loved before  and quite deeply to which geinan  responds always with those who would  only be temporary it's true that picard  has had a handful of dalliances in trek  and the most obvious candidate is  renegade archaeologist bosh whom he met  in captain's holiday hupacard clearly  held a deep and passionate attraction  towards but as gainan said vosh was  never a good match because she was  always on the move and picard's first  love was starfleet their most haunting  exchange is when she tells picard that  he should know better than most that  there are worse things than being dead  this is a clear reference to his  previous assimilation by the borg when  they stole his body and mind and used  him as an instrument of destruction  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies and tv shows are  coming soon subscribe to our youtube  channel and hit the bell so you don't  miss a single one  [Music]
 

